1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,500
You cannot protect what you cannot see. Biodiversity data has blind spots.
WildGap turns those missing observations into one useful action: a safe field mission.

2
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:27,500
Search any city, park, or neighbourhood. Choose a two, five, or ten kilometre area.
WildGap divides it into comparable H3 cells and combines two open data sources.

3
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:48,000
Each cell compares the latest twelve completed months of GBIF coverage
with the preceding three-year baseline using a transparent score.

4
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:04,000
This is survey priority—not habitat health, wildlife abundance,
population decline, or a diagnosis of ecological cause.

5
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:18,500
When comparable baseline data is insufficient, WildGap suppresses the ranking.
Every result includes confidence and source timestamps.

6
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:33,000
The map makes spatial patterns visible. The ranked list gives keyboard
and screen-reader users equal access to every candidate.

7
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:47,500
Open-Meteo adds climate context and ranks comfortable field days.
Choose a date to create a repeatable sixty-minute survey mission.

8
00:01:47,500 --> 00:02:03,500
The mission card works on a phone, prints cleanly, and shares without an account.
After the outing, add public observation evidence and mark it complete.

9
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:21,500
WildGap uses Next.js, TypeScript, MapLibre, H3, GBIF, and Open-Meteo,
with bounded requests, caching, and an explicitly labeled Winnipeg snapshot.

10
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:38,000
This is the pre-pilot cut. Before submission, real Winnipeg evidence
and measured results from two uncoached testers must replace this segment.

11
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,300
Nature has blind spots.
Let’s go find them.
